        "QuestionText": "<b>The team leader</b><p>After weeks of intense preparation, Steve was thrilled when his team was awarded a performance bonus for exceeding their sales target. The recognition felt satisfying—a tangible reward for the long hours he'd poured into work and the many nights he'd stayed late fine-tuning his pitch. Steve's teammate Max had also played an important role, stepping in at a pivotal moment to offer a key tip that helped close the biggest deal. <br><br>The director called Steve to discuss the bonus and said: \"You guys did great. As you know, since you are the team leader, the usual split is for you to get 70% of the bonus and for Max to get 30%. However, the final decision is yours.\" <br><br>Now Steve faced a choice. He could stick with the usual split, which already gave him most of the bonus. Or he could take from Max’s 30%, arguing that his leadership and effort justified a bigger share. But he could also choose to give Max a larger portion, knowing that Max's contribution—though small—had come at a crucial moment. What should he do?</p>",
