        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>A Windfall Decision</strong><br>\nEmma had always enjoyed playing the lottery, even though she never expected to win. But one evening, after buying a ticket on a whim, she was stunned to see her numbers appear on the screen—she had won. It wasn’t a life-changing sum, but it was a nice, unexpected bonus.&nbsp;<br><br></p>\n\n<p>A few days later, as Emma was thinking about what to do with the money, she came across a crowdfunding campaign for a local community center that provided meals and after-school programs for kids from low-income families. The center was struggling to keep up with demand, and the organizers were trying to raise enough funds to stay open through the winter.<br>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Emma knew she had complete control over her lottery winnings. No one expected her to share them, and no one would know if she chose to keep the entire amount. But she also knew that even a small donation could make a real difference to the community center. She could give away part of her winnings—or even all of them—but how much should she actually contribute?&nbsp;</p>\nClick to write the question text",
